1: # The actual source-zero native return and its first diagonal flop
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.60 after complete root and independent checks audits,
4: 17 November 2026. This note retains the complete family
5: of RG.1--7, including its ordinary and cap closing arrows. The earlier
6: algebra-only attempt remains in
7: \path{notes/part3_source_zero_double_loop_diagnostic.md}. The new
8: geometric step is DZ.1: an actual collar coordinate arrow closes the
9: native single-star product, and its literal pullback closes the
10: doubled three-curve plumbing. DZ.4 identifies the actual square in
11: the relative diffeomorphism group. No individual source-zero class
12: is assigned.
13: 
14: The source, scalar and cap data are
15: \[
16:  Y^2=X^3+(1-2v)X^2+TX+v^2,\quad G=(0,v),\quad
17:  q=T-(v^2-2v),\quad \lambda=v^N\lambda_0,
18: \]
19: \[
20:  (a,b,m)=(1,2,4p)\ \text{or}\ (2,1,4p-1),\quad
21:  N=km,\quad p,k\ge1,\quad n=\pm1 .
22: \]
23: The fan is initially the fan of the two triangles in RG.2a. The
24: physical circle is the original positive phase of \(V\). The opposite
25: diagonal fan is its reflection \(J\mathcal T\). Every source, root,
26: Cartier line, finite character, full \(F_0\), and integer annular
27: cochain is the one in RG/K49. There is no additional logarithmic disc
28: at either colliding finite site.
29: 
30: ## DZ.1. The literal parameter seam on all three edges
31: 
32: Take a small positive \(v_*\), and first parameterize one positive
33: turn by \(v=v_*e^{2\pi i\tau}\), \(0\leq\tau\leq1\). Choose one
34: \(q\)-disc which excludes the other moving nodal value for this
35: compact parameter circle. Its full period maps are
36: \[
37:  h:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha V,q),\qquad
38:  B:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(gZ,\eta qV,q),                 \tag{DZ.1}
39: \]
40: where all four coefficients are the actual nowhere-zero holomorphic
41: functions of \(q\) and the cut parameter. Their windings on the
42: double loop, later parameterized by \(v=v_*e^{2i\theta}\), are
43: \[
44:  \begin{pmatrix}\operatorname{wind}\kappa&
45:                  \operatorname{wind}g\\
46:                  \operatorname{wind}\alpha&
47:                  \operatorname{wind}\eta\end{pmatrix}
48:        =2\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&N-2\end{pmatrix}.    \tag{DZ.2}
49: \]
50: The functions themselves, rather than only these integers, are kept
51: below.
52: 
53: Put \(L_0=nk\). The actual forward parameter arrow for one turn is
54: \[
55:  R_{\rm ord}^{(1)}:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(Z,q^{L_0}V,q). \tag{DZ.3}
56: \]
57: It translates the fan indices by \((0,L_0)\). Applying the actual
58: period \(B^{-L_0}\) returns each chosen cone representative to its
59: original index and leaves the bounded map
60: \[
61:  E_v:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto(g(q,v)^{-L_0}Z,
62:                          \eta(q,v)^{-L_0}V,q).     \tag{DZ.4}
63: \]
64: This is an equality of quotient arrows, not a substitution of a
65: bounded multiplier for the original period.
66: 
67: There are holomorphic-in-\(q\), smooth-in-\(\tau\) logarithms
68: \(G=\log g\), \(H=\log\eta\) on the cut disc times interval. Choose a
69: smooth real function \(\chi\), zero on an initial parameter collar and
70: one on a terminal parameter collar. Define the actual local maps
71: \[
72:  C^{(1)}_\tau:(Z,V,q)\longmapsto
73:        (A_\tau Z,D_\tau V,q),\qquad
74:  A_\tau=e^{L_0\chi G},\quad D_\tau=e^{L_0\chi H}.    \tag{DZ.5}
75: \]
76: They have explicit inverses obtained by negating the exponents.
77: On the terminal collar they are exactly
78: \((Z,V)\mapsto(g^{L_0}Z,\eta^{L_0}V)\), independently of the chosen logarithm
79: branches. Thus, on the two parameter collars identified by the
80: original forward arrow,
81: \[
82:                      C_{\rm end}E_v=C_{\rm start}=1. \tag{DZ.6}
83: \]
84: This equality holds as a map in \(q\) and the collar parameter, so
85: all parameter and spatial derivatives match at the seam. Using only
86: the reference values \(g_*,\eta_*\) would not supply this collar
87: identity; (DZ.5) uses the varying functions.
88: 
89: Every multiplier in (DZ.1) and (DZ.5) depends only on the unchanged
90: coordinates \(q,v\). Consequently \(C^{(1)}_\tau\) commutes literally
91: with \(h,B\), and with the physical circle. It therefore descends
92: to the actual quotient neighbourhood. On the two triangle charts its
93: maps are
94: \[
95:  \begin{split}
96:  T^+:(x_0,x_1,x_2)&\longmapsto
97:        ((AD)^{-1}x_0,Ax_1,Dx_2),\\
98:  T^-:(y_0,y_1,y_2)&\longmapsto
99:        (ADy_0,D^{-1}y_1,A^{-1}y_2).
100:  \end{split}                                          \tag{DZ.7}
101: \]
102: Their three-coordinate products remain \(q\), so the maps extend
103: through both vertices and every toric boundary. In the six named
104: charts \((a_0,b_0,c_0)\), \((u_0,v_0,w_0)\), the three transitions are
105: \[
106:  \begin{array}{c|ccc}
107:  &u_0&v_0&w_0\\ \hline
108:  C_0&\kappa\alpha b_0c_0&\alpha^{-1}a_0b_0&
109:                                       \kappa^{-1}b_0^{-1}\\
110:  C_1&g\eta b_0c_0&\eta^{-1}c_0^{-1}&g^{-1}a_0c_0\\
111:  C_2&a_0^{-1}&a_0b_0&a_0c_0 .
112:  \end{array}                                          \tag{DZ.8}
113: \]
114: Substitution of (DZ.7) on either side gives the same factors
115: \((AD,D^{-1},A^{-1})\) in all three rows. This verifies the common
116: edge and corner maps, retaining all four complete units. In
117: particular (DZ.5) changes none of the coefficients in (DZ.8).
118: The vertex charts defined by (DZ.5) are now periodic by (DZ.6).
119: In particular the diagonal native star is already a literal product
120: for this single turn: its two charts satisfy
121: \(z'=z^{-1},c'_i=zc_i\), the \(C_2\) row of (DZ.8), with no
122: parameter-dependent transition. This assertion uses no
123: contraction of the other two curve tubes.
124: 
125: We now specify, rather than independently choose, the doubled
126: seam. On \(0\leq u\leq2\), write \(u=j+\tau\), \(j=0,1\), and put
127: \[
128:               C^{(2)}_{j+\tau}
129:                     =C^{(1)}_\tau E_{v(\tau)}^{-j}. \tag{DZ.8a}
130: \]
131: Near the intermediate seam the two formulas both equal \(E_v^{-1}\);
132: they agree on collars with every derivative by (DZ.6). At the last
133: seam the product is \(E_v^{-2}\), which cancels the doubled
134: forward arrow after the literal period \(B^{-2L_0}\).
135: Every factor in (DZ.8a) commutes with the full periods, the physical
136: circle, and all rows of (DZ.8). Thus these closed doubled charts
137: retain exactly (DZ.2). The double native-star product so obtained
138: is precisely the pullback of the single product under
139: \(u\bmod2\mapsto u\bmod1\): the factor \(E_v^{-j}\) is the
140: transition from the \(j\)-th raw sheet to its first-sheet
141: representative. There is no independently selected second-loop
142: normal framing.
143: 
144: Put \(L=2L_0=2nk\). The double cap retains the actual forward map
145: \(h_\infty^L\) of RG.2,
146: where \(h_\infty=1-T_0(v)q_\infty\), and the full root/norm
147: cochain. Its overlap square remains
148: \[
149:                    F_{\rm end}R_{\rm ord}
150:                          =R_{\rm cap}F_{\rm start}.  \tag{DZ.9}
151: \]
152: The local arrows (DZ.5) and (DZ.8a) are coordinate maps on the cusp
153: neighbourhood, not replacement global source or cap maps. To choose
154: parallel transport compatible with their boundary product, extend
155: its time-dependent velocity from a collar using a cutoff in a
156: slightly larger cusp neighbourhood. This is a smooth vector field
157: on the already constructed compact family and has a complete flow
158: for the compact parameter interval. Outside that neighbourhood use
159: the original family connection and its literal cap arrow.
160: The two vector fields are joined in their affine space. This
161: constructs actual global transport with the prescribed cusp
162: boundary; its endpoint is the physical return formed with the
163: inverse of the unchanged global closing arrow.
164: 
165: ## DZ.2. A closed ordinary plumbing product
166: 
167: Work in the doubled closed charts of DZ.1, and suppress their
168: tildes only after the explicit pullback map (DZ.8a). At the second vertex use
169: \((u_0,\eta v_0,\kappa w_0)\), with the same initial constant
170: transformation at the reference fibre. The ordered normal matrices
171: are literally
172: \[
173:  D_0=\operatorname{diag}(\eta/\alpha,\kappa\alpha),\quad
174:  D_1=\operatorname{diag}(\kappa/g,g\eta),\quad
175:  D_2=\operatorname{diag}(\eta,\kappa).                \tag{DZ.10}
176: \]
177: Their common determinant winding is \(2t\), \(t=N-1\), and their
178: first diagonal windings are
179: \[
180:  l_0=2(N+a-2),\qquad l_1=2(1-b),\qquad
181:  l_2=2(N-2).                                        \tag{DZ.11}
182: \]
183: The based ratio of each diagonal entry is its displayed angular
184: factor times the exponential of its actual based periodic
185: logarithm. This follows by subtracting its winding from the full
186: nonzero loop, on the same \(q\)-disc.
187: 
188: For every integer \(d\) use the actual based quaternion contraction
189: \[
190:  H_d(s,\theta)=
191:   (\sin\vartheta(s)e^{id\theta}+j\cos\vartheta(s))
192:   (\sin\vartheta(s)+j\cos\vartheta(s))^{-1},          \tag{DZ.12}
193: \]
194: where \(\vartheta\) is smooth, constant at \(0,\pi/2\) near the two
195: ends. Thus \(H_d(0,\theta)=1\), \(H_d(1,\theta)=e^{id\theta}\), and
196: both parameter endpoints are one. For
197: \(Q=n_0+j\overline{n_1}\), set
198: \[
199:               T_i(Q)=H_t Q H_{l_i-t}.               \tag{DZ.13}
200: \]
201: At its second radial end this has the two complex factors
202: \((e^{il_i\theta},e^{i(2t-l_i)\theta})\), exactly the angular part
203: of \(D_{i,*}^{-1}D_i\). Multiply by the diagonal exponentials of
204: the retained periodic unit logarithms, with a radial cutoff zero
205: at the first end and one at the second. The resulting
206: \(\mathcal K_i\) is based and periodic and satisfies
207: \[
208:  \mathcal K_i(0,\theta)=1,\quad
209:  \mathcal K_i(1,\theta)=D_{i,*}^{-1}D_i,\quad
210:                    zD_{i,*}\mathcal K_i(1,\theta)=zD_i. \tag{DZ.14}
211: \]
212: On the tube of the \(i\)-th curve use
213: \((z,n)\mapsto(z,\mathcal K_i(s(|z|),\theta)n)\).
214: The last equality is its exact second-vertex collar equality;
215: near the first vertex it is the identity. The three curve interiors
216: are disjoint, so the three maps glue to the two vertex maps.
217: 
218: For the original, nonlinear germs retain the full normal rescaling
219: \(R_\tau(z,n)=(z,\tau n)\). The conjugation
220: \(R_\tau^{-1}FR_\tau\) extends smoothly to \(\tau=0\) with the
221: zero-section map and its invertible normal derivative. A common
222: small tube works on the compact parameter interval. Integrating
223: the cutoff velocity of this isotopy restores the actual higher
224: normal terms, with the vertex collars protected. These isotopies
225: are periodic in the closed charts, and the maps (DZ.8a) carry them
226: back to the original family with its seam (DZ.6).
227: Finally carry the rounded boundary by the same collar isotopy.
228: This is an actual based oriented ordinary product of the full
229: three-curve plumbing, not merely of its normal bundles.
230: 
231: ## DZ.3. Actual circle traces, derivative and the diagonal right integer
232: 
233: The maps (DZ.8a) commute with the physical circle. Therefore that
234: circle has precisely the original vertex weights
235: \((-1,0,1)\), \((1,-1,0)\) in the closed charts. In the ordered
236: curve normal coordinates its tangent weights and normal differences
237: are
238: \[
239:                   w=(0,1,-1),\qquad d=(-2,-1,-1).
240: \]
241: The ordinary product (DZ.13) carries its normal action to
242: \[
243:  Q\longmapsto
244:  (H_t e^{-iw_i\phi/2}H_t^{-1})Q
245:  (H_{l_i-t}^{-1}e^{id_i\phi/2}H_{l_i-t}).            \tag{DZ.15}
246: \]
247: The retained diagonal unit exponentials conjugate this literal
248: formula and give a based homotopy back to it, with the normal metric
249: transported. Normal rescaling carries the higher derivatives as in
250: DZ.2. Both parameter ends and every radial-end physical orbit are
251: closed; the vertex orbit maps are independent of the parameter.
252: 
253: Here is the actual degree calculation with its arrows. On the
254: positive source path put \(U=\cos\chi+j\sin\chi\) and
255: \(E_i=e^{iw_i\phi/2}\). The moved source Hopf representative is
256: \(E_i^{-1}UE_i\). At the normal section \(q_H=1\), the trace is
257: \[
258:  E_i^{-1}UE_i H_t E_i^{-1}H_t^{-1}U^{-1}
259:               H_{l_i-t}^{-1}e^{id_i\phi/2}H_{l_i-t}. \tag{DZ.16}
260: \]
261: For (DZ.12), direct differentiation at the inverse images of
262: \(-i\) gives
263: \(\deg(H_diH_d^{-1})=-d\) and
264: \(\deg(H_d^{-1}iH_d)=d\), in the path--parameter orientation.
265: The factor \(UiU^{-1}\) has degree zero. The positive quaternion
266: volume gives degree \(c e\) for an axis of degree \(e\) and angle
267: \(c\phi/2\). The exact Maurer product boundary terms vanish on
268: the radial ends and the constant central-sign angle ends; the
269: parameter faces cancel. Thus (DZ.16) has normal-meridian degree
270: \[
271:                     I_i=t w_i+d_i(l_i-t).            \tag{DZ.17}
272: \]
273: For the derivative use the universal covering group of stable
274: \(GL^+\), with its integral third class identified by the lifted
275: polar deformation. The chain-rule identity is retained in that
276: group. A left quaternion factor contributes its degree and a right
277: factor its negative. The base rotation has only the \(\phi\)
278: variable. At \(2\pi\) the base and normal Spin endpoints are both
279: \((-1)^{w_i}\), so their product is one. The resulting full
280: closed stable derivative has degree
281: \[
282:                     W_i=t w_i-d_i(l_i-t).            \tag{DZ.18}
283: \]
284: These computations use the same actual endpoint caps as CT.3:
285: take generic normal sections with all vertex coordinates nonzero,
286: connect them by paths interpolating squared moduli and phases in
287: the vertex links, and cap each parameter-independent end by
288: \(D^2_\theta\times S^1_\phi\), constant in its disc variable.
289: The circle changes only phases and preserves the axis-tube
290: inequalities. The vertex traces factor through dimension two.
291: Thus no extra vertex meridian or third derivative class occurs.
292: The change from the coordinate tangent frame to the ambient
293: stable frame extends through each annulus times its normal
294: four-disc, whose third cohomology is zero; its pairing on each
295: such capped trace is zero. This is the same exact frame arrow
296: used in ST.2, now on the closed charts constructed in DZ.1.
297: 
298: Let \(V\) be the actual ordinary-product variation, \(\delta\)
299: the actual ambient stable derivative, and \(D_i,m_i\) the
300: oriented relative cycles and normal meridians. For
301: \(\gamma_j=a_j-a_0=\partial x_j\), the positive-circle boundary
302: identity \(\partial\Delta=-\Delta\partial\) gives
303: \[
304:              \Delta x_j=w_jD_j-w_0D_0 .
305: \]
306: The literal action prism and the chain-rule/Stokes prism then give
307: \[
308:  V(w_je_j-w_0e_0)=I_je_j-I_0e_0,\qquad
309:  w_j\delta_j-w_0\delta_0=W_j-W_0.                    \tag{DZ.19}
310: \]
311: The return fixes the common boundary by the actual product in
312: DZ.2; its integral meridians generate \(H_3\), and \(H_2\) of the
313: common exterior is zero. These are exactly the groups in the
314: three-curve complement of K29, since the curves and their
315: neighbourhood have been carried by the explicit ordinary product.
316: They justify both prism identities as identities of the actual
317: return.
318: 
319: Substituting (DZ.11) gives
320: \[
321:  \begin{split}
322:  I&=(-2(N+2a-3),\,2N+2b-4,\,-2N+4),\\
323:  W&=(2(N+2a-3),\,2-2b,\,-2).
324:  \end{split}                                          \tag{DZ.20}
325: \]
326: In particular \(w_0=0,w_2=-1\) yield
327: \[
328:                   V_{22}=2N-4,\qquad
329:                   \delta_2=2N+4a-4.                 \tag{DZ.21}
330: \]
331: K31's actual ambient-to-single-star product frame map has third
332: class \(e_2\). Carrying that map by the based product above retains
333: its derivative correction \(-V_{22}\); it is not an identification
334: of two unmarked frames. Hence the actual ordinary relative
335: integers satisfy
336: \[
337:  2n_2=\delta_2-V_{22},\qquad m_2=-V_{22}-n_2,
338:  \quad\text{so}\quad
339:            (m_2,n_2)=(-2N+4-2a,\,2a).               \tag{DZ.22}
340: \]
341: 
342: ## DZ.4. The native Cox product and the actual flop
343: 
344: The diagonal native star in the closed charts is literally
345: \[
346:  (z,c_0,c_1)=(a_0,b_0,c_0),\qquad
347:  (z',c'_0,c'_1)=(u_0,v_0,w_0),\qquad
348:                   z'=z^{-1},\quad c'_i=zc_i.
349: \]
350: Its Hopf coordinate is
351: \(q_H=U(z)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\).
352: The native-to-ordinary boundary arrow is, with the full unit
353: homotopy retained,
354: \[
355:  q_H\longmapsto
356:        (U H_t U^{-1})q_H H_{l_2-t}.                 \tag{DZ.23}
357: \]
358: Its left factor has degree \(2t\) on the Hopf-oriented sphere
359: times the parameter, by the exact Maurer boundary integral of
360: OP.21--23 with exponent \(t\). Its right factor depends on only
361: the radial coordinate and parameter and has third degree zero.
362: Extending the inverse factors over \(D^3\times I\) by the
363: literal cylinder retraction of OR.2 gives endpoint degrees
364: \((-2t,0)\). Consequently the ordinary relative return is the
365: native return followed by the actual pure-left map \(L^{-2t}\).
366: There is no disc-supported factor: these coefficient maps extend
367: over \(D^3\times D^4\). In particular the native right integer is
368: unchanged, and the complete native pair is
369: \[
370:                         (m_2^{\rm nat},n_2^{\rm nat})
371:                                       =(2-2a,\,2a). \tag{DZ.24}
372: \]
373: 
374: The single and doubled seam maps themselves also extend through
375: the flop. In Cox
376: coordinates with auxiliary weights \((1,1,-1,-1)\), take the
377: chart \(z=a_1/a_0,c_i^{\rm chart}=a_0c_i\).
378: For the multipliers \(A,D\) in (DZ.5) or (DZ.8a), the literal Cox lift is
379: \[
380:  (a_0,a_1,c_0,c_1)\longmapsto
381:        (a_0,(AD)^{-1}a_1,Ac_0,Dc_1).               \tag{DZ.25}
382: \]
383: Here the actual height is \(q=a_0a_1c_0c_1\), which this map
384: preserves. Thus even for the full \(q\)-dependent multipliers its
385: inverse is obtained by replacing \(A,D\) by \(A^{-1},D^{-1}\)
386: at the same \(q\); no implicit inverse or constant-unit
387: replacement is needed.
388: It commutes with the auxiliary action, the physical circle and
389: all period translates. It gives (DZ.7) on the original star and
390: extends to the other small resolution by its other quotient
391: chart. Transport the Hermitian metric by this map, then use its
392: positive radial normalization on the common link. Thus the
393: two native product links and their parameter seams are carried
394: by the same actual Cox map, with all its nonunit moduli retained.
395: On each free period representative this is a literal map;
396: equivariance extends it to the whole periodic orbit of curves.
397: 
398: The common-link arrow and its oriented exterior extension are
399: therefore the same explicit maps
400: \[
401:  \tau(h,q_H)=(q_H^{-1}hq_H,q_H),\qquad
402:  T(y,q_H)=(q_H^{-1}yq_H,q_H) .
403: \]
404: The unrestricted four-weight identity of FF.1/SF.8 intertwines
405: them with (DZ.25), and normal rescaling carries the full
406: \(q\)-dependent unit germs on both small resolutions. The
407: physical cap and exterior family are unchanged outside these
408: matched cusp charts. The diagonal flip gives exactly
409: \(J\mathcal T\) from \(\mathcal T\), including the integral
410: period indexing.
411: 
412: We first identify the actual relative square, before applying the
413: flop formula. Let \(f\) be the positive one-turn return on the
414: exterior of the single native star, using precisely the single
415: product following (DZ.8). Choose a global family connection
416: compatible with this product on its boundary. Its literal pullback
417: to the degree-two parameter cover has relative return \(f^2\):
418: the first sheet has endpoint \(f\), and the second uses the same
419: connection and boundary product under the transition \(E_v^{-1}\)
420: in (DZ.8a). The full forward arrows compose as
